---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { rooms, roomWorks } from '../lib/exhibitions';
const total = rooms.reduce(
  (n, room) => n + roomWorks(room).length + room.relatedItems.length,
  0,
);
---

<Layout
  title="Notes from the curator — nnxyz"
  description="How the rooms, the gallery text, the audio tour and the map of this collection were made."
>
  <article class="text-page notes-page">
    <h1>Notes from the curator</h1>
    <p>
      The rooms of this site are unsigned, like the wall text in a museum. This
      page is the exception: a few notes on how the collection was hung and
      written, and on what the text does and does not claim.
    </p>

    <h2>Who the curator is</h2>
    <p>
      The curator is an AI assistant working in conversation with the collector.
      The assistant looked at every work, proposed the rooms, wrote the text and
      the captions, and read its own drafts back in page order for repetition.
      The collector set the brief, answered the questions the hanging raised,
      approved the structure and the pilot room, chose the voice for the audio
      tour, and wrote the statement on the front page. Those are the only words
      of the collector's on the site. Everything else on the walls was written
      by the assistant from the works and their archived records, and corrected
      where a second look showed it wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>How the rooms were hung</h2>
    <p>
      Each collection is a wing. New Nifty on the Block, the largest at 303
      works, is split into three dated parts because the drawing changes as the
      year goes on. Inside each wing the works were laid out on dated contact
      sheets and grouped by what they look like, not by their titles or numbers:
      black silhouettes with wavy marks, heads built from boxes, masks buried in
      scribble. Every work hangs in exactly one room, rooms hold twenty to
      thirty works, and each room is hung in the order the works were drawn, so
      a walk through a room is also a walk through a few weeks or months of
      drawing. Two exceptions were the collector's decisions: the two Rawblocks
      landscapes hang as a coda rather than being forced into a room, and one
      greeting by Scrimshaw hangs as a related work at the end of Occasions and
      Figures. Each wing ends on its collection piece where the series has one.
    </p>

    <h2>How the text was written</h2>
    <p>
      The voice is a museum label's: specific, restrained, and about what is on
      the wall. A label says where the eyes are and what the beam does to the
      scribble under it. It does not say what the artist meant, and it does not
      tell you how to feel. A possible mood is offered as a reading, and left
      there. Stock praise and repeated metaphors were cut in an editing pass
      over all twenty-eight rooms; so were most of the numbers, which belong in
      the details panel of each work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every number that remains comes from software reading the archive, not
      from looking: stroke counts, dates, the order of works. Dates and times
      are UTC, from the archived creation timestamps, because this is a gallery
      for visitors in every time zone; the artwork pages add your local time
      beside the UTC instant. Where two works share a title, or one is a fork of
      another, the text follows the stable drawing identifiers rather than the
      names.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rooms were written one at a time, each after the assistant had looked
      at every work in the room at full size and as one wall. One room, Black
      Silhouettes, was written first as a pilot and shown to the collector
      before the rest. The text was then read as a whole, in page order, and
      edited for the tics that creep into {rooms.length} rooms written in a few
      days.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawings themselves</h2>
    <p>
      The Nifty Ink drawings are reconstructed from their archived brush
      strokes, so each one is shown as a native vector image and can be watched
      being drawn, stroke by stroke, with the same order, widths, colors and
      transparency the archive records. The archive does not record how fast the
      artist drew, so playback timing is reconstructed and labelled as such. The
      Abstract works survive only as published images from the collector's
      earlier gallery; they are shown as stills, with their original files
      available to download. The complete archived records remain downloadable
      from every artwork page.
    </p>

    <h2>The audio tour, the screen saver and the map</h2>
    <p>
      The gallery text was written in segments tied to works so that it could
      also be heard. Each room's segments were read by a synthetic voice chosen
      by the collector from samples, with a short list of spoken forms for names
      that are written one way and said another. The tour brings each work on
      screen as its segment plays; silent works hold for a few seconds. The
      screen saver shows one work at a time with the standalone caption every
      work was given. The map draws the collection as a building on three
      floors, with rooms sized by how many works they hold and a door from each
      room into the next, which is how a visitor would walk it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is not a catalogue of either artist's complete work; it shows the{' '}
      {total} works in one collection. It is not research into the artist: no
      influences, intentions or quotations appear anywhere, because none were
      sourced. And it is not a verdict on quality. Measurements such as stroke
      count describe how a drawing was recorded, not how good it is or how long
      it took.
    </p>
    <p>
      If something on a wall is wrong, it can be corrected: every reading is
      kept as a record with the image it was written from, and a correction
      replaces the text without touching the archive.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/about/">About the collection →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/">Return to the collection →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
